  <data name="Label16.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Wm - Soil storage capacity. 
It can be understood as the capacity of 
the soil to store rain water generating 
little or no runoff. Normal values range 
between 50 and 1000, being higher 
for sandy soils.

b - Parameter that drives the 
correlation shape between storage and 
saturation. It controls the 
separation of runoff to saturation of 
the soil storage capacity. In terms of 
model results, the increase in 
parameter b makes the hydrogram more 
"nervous". The values are usually around 
0.12, but can reach over 1.6. The 
calibration of the parameter b is made to 
adjust the observed and calculated peak 
flows mainly during small floods.

Kbas - parameter that controls the flow 
during the dry season. A higher value 
implies greater groundwater flow. Typical 
values ranging from 0.05 to 5.

Kint - This parameter controls the amount 
of water from the soil layer which is 
disposedunder-surface. The most suitable
values of Kint float around 4-40 mm.day-1.

XL - controls the shape of the reduction 
curve of the intermediate drainage or 
subsurface soil. Values revolve around 0.67.

CAP - controls the flow of underground
reservoir to the surface layer of soil over 
the time interval.Generally the possibility 
of returning groundwater to the soil layer 
is discarded, i.e., CAP is equal to zero.

Wc - residual storage parameters. Limits 
the flow sub-surface and underground. 
Usual values stay around 0.10.

CS - parameter for calibration of surface
spread on the cells.

CI - calibration parameter for the 
sub-surface spread on cells.

CB - is the delay parameter of the 
underground reservoir. Its value is 
normally between 1200 and 8000 hours.

QB - values range around 0.01.
</value>
  </data>
